Remote work and freelancing for overseas clients is now a normal part of Sri Lankan working life — and the tax treatment of that income has its own rules worth understanding before you file.

## The foreign-service concession

Income from providing services to people or businesses outside Sri Lanka, remitted through the banking system, can qualify for concessionary final tax treatment rather than being taxed at your regular progressive rate.

## Foreign tax credits

If tax was already withheld overseas on the same income, a credit may be available against your Sri Lankan liability, so you're not taxed twice on the same rupee.

## The mistakes that cost people the most

The two most common: not remitting through a bank in a way that qualifies for the concession, and not keeping documentation of foreign tax paid — both of which can mean paying more than necessary. Salli's engine applies these rules from your actual ledger entries, so the qualifying treatment isn't something you have to remember to claim.
